id: rust.tower
kind: rust
title: Tower layers
aliases: tower, middleware, layer
tags: http, resilience, architecture
questions:
  - where do timeouts, retries and rate limits belong in a Rust service?
invariant: Middleware is a Tower layer wrapping a Service, so the same policy types apply to inbound and outbound calls.
answer_30s: Tower layers compose request policy -- timeout, retry, concurrency limit, rate limit, tracing, load shedding -- around any service, on either side of the connection.
answer_2m: |
  The architecture payoff is that policy lives outside business logic and is
  testable on its own; the subtle part is ordering, since the outermost layer
  sees the request first (a timeout outside a retry bounds the whole sequence,
  inside it bounds each attempt). The same builder pattern protects outbound
  calls: a client wrapped with timeout, retry and a concurrency limit is how an
  unreliable dependency stops being able to take the service down with it.
when_to_use:
  - policy that applies to many routes or many outbound calls
avoid_when:
  - per-route business rules (keep those in handlers)
minimal_example: |
  use tower::{ServiceBuilder, timeout::TimeoutLayer};

  let client = ServiceBuilder::new()
      .layer(TimeoutLayer::new(Duration::from_millis(300)))
      .layer(tower::limit::ConcurrencyLimitLayer::new(32))
      .service(hyper_client);
  let _ = client;
failure_modes:
  - a layer order that silently disables a policy (retry outside timeout)
  - a retry layer on non-idempotent routes
related:
  - rust.axum
  - rust.retry
  - rust.bounded-concurrency
  - design.rate-limiting
sources:
  - https://docs.rs/tower/latest/tower/
